// `packages/platform/example/counter.wac`, rewritten — the first vision file with a page in it.
//
// The original is 69 lines and builds its document by concatenating eight string literals. wac has
// JSX; the example does not use it, and neither does any other page in the tree.

// `Out` rather than `Sys`, which is the twenty-second file to do that and the reason the bundle is
// nearly unused: this page needs one capability and there is no reason to hand it eight. The entry
// point signature the launcher fixes is `page(Core, Cli, Page)`, so what a `page` *receives* is not
// this function's to choose — but what it passes on is.
import { Out, Page, Event, Click, Typed, Quit } from "std";
import { Node } from "core";
import { itoa } from "@/packages/fmt";

/**
 * The whole page, as a tree rather than as eight quoted fragments.
 *
 * The original:
 *
 *     page.render(
 *       "<p>Clicks: <b id=\"n\">0</b></p>" +
 *       "<button id=\"up\">+1</button> " +
 *       …
 *     ).wait();
 *
 * Escaped quotes on every attribute, no structure the compiler can see, and the closing tags are
 * the author's problem. `spec/spec/jsx.md` exists and says a tag is *"a function in scope"*, with
 * `render` producing the markup — so the language already has the form this wants.
 */
Node document(i32 n) {
  return <div>
    <p>Clicks: <b id="n">{itoa(n)}</b></p>
    <button id="up">+1</button>
    <button id="down">-1</button>
    <button id="reset">reset</button>
    <p><label @"for"="echo">Type here: </label><input id="echo" size="24" /></p>
    <p id="said" class="meta" data-role="echo"></p>
    <p><button id="quit">quit</button></p>
  </div>;
}

/**
 * The event loop, with the `if`/`else if` chain on a string replaced by a match on a union.
 *
 * The original compares `e.kind == "input"` and then `e.id == "up"`, `"down"`, `"reset"`, `"quit"`
 * — five string comparisons against literals that also appear as `id` attributes above, with
 * nothing relating the two. A typo in either place is a button that stops working.
 */
export async i32 page(Out out, Page ui) {
  await ui.title("counter");
  await ui.render(document(0));
  await ui.on("button", "click");
  await ui.on("#echo", "input");

  i32 n = 0;
  while (true) {
    match (await ui.nextEvent()) {
      Typed(id, value): {
        await ui.setText("said", value == "" ? "" : "you typed: \{value}");
      }
      Click(id): {
        if (id == "quit") {
          out.log("counted to \{n}");
          return 0;
        }
        if (id == "up")    { n = n + 1; }
        if (id == "down")  { n = n - 1; }
        if (id == "reset") {
          n = 0;
          await ui.setValue("echo", "");
          await ui.setText("said", "");
        }
        await ui.setText("n", itoa(n));
      }
      Quit: { return 0; }
    }
  }
}

// ## 1. `render` takes a string, so JSX stops at the capability boundary
//
// The shipped `Page.render` is `fn[Pending<bool>(string)]`. JSX produces a `Node`. So the one file
// in the repository that uses JSX — `packages/platform/example/page.wac`, 68 lines whose purpose is
// to demonstrate the feature — **writes its own renderer**:
//
//     string out = "<" + tag;
//     for (i32 i = 0; i < attrs.len(); i++) { … }
//     for (i32 i = 0; i < kids.len(); i++) { out = out + render(kids[i]); }
//     return out + "</" + tag + ">";
//
// Thirteen lines of string concatenation, in the example, below the JSX it exists to show off. A
// second page would write it again or import it from an example.
//
// `ui.render` here takes a `Node`, which is the whole change, and it is a change to the *capability*
// rather than to the language. The alternative is a `render(Node)` in a library, which is what
// `page.wac` has by accident — and then every page pays a string round trip to hand markup to a host
// that is going to parse it back into a tree.
//
// **The interesting part is that this is the same shape as `In.read` and `Clock`:** a capability
// whose type is narrower than the thing the language can express, so the program does the
// conversion. It is the third instance and the first where the loss is a whole feature rather than
// a distinction.
//
// ## 2. `Event` is a struct with a `kind` string, and the ids are unrelated to the markup
//
// The original's loop is `e.kind == "input"` then `e.id == "up"`, `"down"`, `"reset"`, `"quit"` —
// five comparisons against string literals that also appear as `id` attributes twenty lines above,
// with nothing connecting the two. Rename a button and the page compiles and stops working.
//
// The union above fixes half of it: `Typed` and `Click` are arms rather than a `kind` field, so the
// *kind* comparison cannot be misspelled and a new kind of event makes the match inexhaustive. The
// **ids are still strings** and still unrelated to the tree, and nothing here can fix that: the id
// is in the document, the comparison is in the loop, and the only thing that could relate them is a
// type generated from the markup. That is a bigger idea than this file and is not proposed.
//
// ## 3. The three page-only constructs got their first users, and one of them earned it
//
// `@"for"`, a quoted tag and a hyphenated attribute were three of the six constructs in
// `GRAMMAR.ebnf` with no code user anywhere — added from `TECHNICAL.md` because the pages have
// them, never written with. Two are here:
//
//   - `data-role="echo"` — a hyphenated attribute. Ordinary HTML, needed the moment anything renders
//     a real document, and it is a fair question why the first page written in nine days is the
//     first to want one.
//   - `<label @"for"="echo">` — the verbatim name, and it is the entry `TECHNICAL.md` leads with.
//     `for` is a keyword and `for` is also the attribute that makes a label clickable, so an escape
//     is not decoration here: without it this line cannot be written at all.
//
// The third, a **quoted tag** — `<"my-widget" />` — still has no user, and a custom element is a
// real thing a page wants. It stays unexercised because this page has no custom elements, which is
// honest rather than an argument.
//
// ## 4. `page` is the entry point and `vision/std` has no story for it
//
// `spec/cli/wac.md`: the launcher hands `Page` to `page` and `Cli` to `main`, and a module may
// export both. `vision/std`'s `Sys` had eight projections and none of them was a page, so a vision
// program could not be interactive. That is the finding this file was written to produce; `Page` is
// the ninth projection now and the README has the rest.
//
// It is deliberately *not* a field on `Sys`, and this function is why: it takes `Out` and `Page`,
// two capabilities, and never wants a bundle. See `../../../QUESTIONS.md` on whether the whole grant
// is a thing that exists.
